# 5. Security & API Hardening

This module protects the entire API rather than exposing new business functionality.

Your API should implement the following layers:

```text
Request
   ↓
CORS
   ↓
Helmet
   ↓
Rate Limiting
   ↓
Authentication
   ↓
Authorization
   ↓
Validation
   ↓
Business Logic
   ↓
Prisma
   ↓
PostgreSQL
```

---

## 5.1 Authentication

Protected endpoints require:

```http
Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
```

### Missing token

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Authentication required"
}
```

HTTP:

```text
401 Unauthorized
```

### Invalid/expired token

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Invalid or expired access token"
}
```

HTTP:

```text
401 Unauthorized
```

---

# 6. Role-Based Authorization

For example:

```http
PATCH /api/v1/documents/:id/verify
```

requires:

```text
ADMIN
LOAN_OFFICER
```

If a customer attempts it:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "You do not have permission to perform this action"
}
```

HTTP:

```text
403 Forbidden
```

---

# 7. Request Validation

Every request body should be validated using Zod.

For example, loan amount:

```json
{
  "requestedAmount": 5000
}
```

If the schema requires a positive amount and the request contains:

```json
{
  "requestedAmount": -500
}
```

the API should return:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Validation failed",
  "errors": [
    {
      "field": "requestedAmount",
      "message": "Requested amount must be greater than zero"
    }
  ]
}
```

HTTP:

```text
400 Bad Request
```

---

# 8. Rate Limiting

Rate limiting should particularly protect:

```text
/auth/login
/auth/register
/auth/verify-otp
/auth/resend-otp
/auth/forgot-password
```

For example, excessive OTP requests should return:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Too many requests. Please try again later."
}
```

HTTP:

```text
429 Too Many Requests
```

---

# 9. Security Headers

You already have:

```ts
app.use(helmet());
```

This should remain enabled.

It helps add security-related HTTP headers to responses.

---

# 10. CORS

You already have CORS configured.

Production should only allow your actual frontend domains.

For example:

```env
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://yourfrontend.com
```

Avoid:

```env
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=*
```

in production when credentials are involved.

---

# 11. File Upload Security

Your document upload should enforce:

```text
Maximum file size
Allowed MIME type
Allowed extension
Authenticated user
Application ownership
```

For your application form:

```text
Allowed:
.docx

Maximum:
10 MB
```

A rejected upload should return:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Only DOCX files are allowed"
}
```

---

# 12. Ownership Checks

This is particularly important for your loan application.

A customer must not be able to do:

```http
GET /api/v1/documents/application/SOMEONE_ELSES_APPLICATION
```

just by knowing the UUID.

The backend must verify:

```text
application.userId === authenticatedUser.userId
```

Otherwise return:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "You do not have access to this application"
}
```

HTTP:

```text
403 Forbidden
```

This same principle should apply to:

* Documents
* Guarantors
* Collateral
* Loan applications
* Loans
* Repayment schedules
* Payments

---

# 13. Sensitive Data Protection

Do not return sensitive information unnecessarily.

For example, a normal user response should not expose:

```text
passwordHash
refreshToken
OTP codes
OTP hashes
internal authentication data
```

An admin response should also only expose information necessary for the administrative task.

---

# 14. Audit Logging

Sensitive operations should create audit records.

Examples:

```text
USER_CREATED
USER_UPDATED
ROLE_CHANGED
USER_SUSPENDED

KYC_APPROVED
KYC_REJECTED

DOCUMENT_VERIFIED
DOCUMENT_REJECTED

LOAN_APPROVED
LOAN_REJECTED
LOAN_DISBURSED

PAYMENT_CREATED
PAYMENT_COMPLETED
```

Example audit record:

```json
{
  "action": "APPROVE",
  "userId": "7b9f3d20-8c51-4e91-8f1d-5e0e1a2b3344",
  "entity": "DOCUMENT",
  "entityId": "d1234567-89ab-4cde-9012-345678901234",
  "ipAddress": "192.168.1.20",
  "userAgent": "Mozilla/5.0",
  "createdAt": "2026-09-28T11:00:00.000Z"
}
```

---

# 15. Standard Error Format

All APIs should follow a consistent structure.

### Success

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Operation completed successfully",
  "data": {}
}
```

### Validation error

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Validation failed",
  "errors": []
}
```

### Authentication error

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Authentication required"
}
```

### Authorization error

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "You do not have permission to perform this action"
}
```

### Not found

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Resource not found"
}
```

### Server error

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Internal server error"
}
```

